grep command
-------------
-Search content in a single file or multiple files.
  GREP=(Global Regular Expression Print)
syntax- grep filename whatToSearch

Example
---------
Create a file with add content
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ vi data1.txt
linux is a easy language.
linux is a os.
python is a programming language.
mysql is database sw.

[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep "linux" data1.txt
linux is a easy language.
linux is a os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

-It find all lines have 'linux' word.

Grep also use in multiple directories
-------------------------------------
-create and copy the data of data1.txt
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ cat data1.txt > data2.txt
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ cat data1.txt > data3.txt

grep chek all the files 'python' word.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep "python" data1.txt data2.txt data3.txt
data1.txt:python is a programming language.
data2.txt:python is a programming language.
data3.txt:python is a programming language.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Search using *.txt 
------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep 'python' *.txt
data1.txt:python is a programming language.
data2.txt:python is a programming language.
data3.txt:python is a programming language.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Search 'python' any file use *
-------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep 'python' *
data1.txt:python is a programming language.
data2.txt:python is a programming language.
data3.txt:python is a programming language.
data4.doc:python is a programming language.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

grep is a case sensitive-means(linux and Linux is diffrent)
-----------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ cat > data4.txt
linux is a os.
Linux is secure os.
^C
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep 'linux' data4.txt
linux is a os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep 'Linux' data4.txt
Linux is secure os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

I want to case insensitive(-i)
--------------------------
grep -i linux data4.txt
- -i (for case insensitive)
- linux (word for search)

[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -i linux data4.txt
linux is a os.
Linux is secure os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

           OR(use linux/'linux'=same)
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -i 'linux' data4.txt
linux is a os.
Linux is secure os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Counts the number of lines containing "linux" in each .txt file.(-c)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -c linux *.txt
data1.txt:2
data2.txt:2
data3.txt:2
data4.txt:1
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Displays matching lines with their line numbers(-n)
---------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -n linux data1.txt
1:linux is a easy language.
2:linux is a os.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Displays lines that do not contain "lunux"(-v)
----------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -v linux data1.txt
python is a programming language.
mysql is database sw.

Displays the names of files containing "linux"(-I)
--------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -I linux data1.txt
linux is a easy language.
linux is a os.

Search exact word(-w)
----------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ cat > data5.txt
python programming
python program
^C
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep program data5.txt
python programming
python program
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ ^C
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

-See it show programming and program word but we need exact word use -w.
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -w program data5.txt
python program
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Searches for the exact word "linux" (case-insensitive) in each .txt file(-iw)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -iw linux data4.txt
linux is a os.
Linux is secure os.

Displays matching lines and 2 lines after each match.(-A 2)
-----------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ cat > data6.txt
a
s
d
w

s

d
s
s
w
linux
d
f
g
h
u
w
e
w
q
^C
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

-A 2 linux(You can use 4 , 5 .... insed of 2)
-----------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -A 2 linux data6.txt
linux
d
f
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$

Displays matching lines and 2 lines before each match.(-B 2 )
-------------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -B 2 linux data6.txt
s
w
linux

Displays matching lines and 2 lines before and after each match.(-C 2)
------------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -C 2 linux data6.txt
s
w
linux
d
f

Searches for lines containing either "python" or "linux".(grep -e "python" -e "linux" data.txt 
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ grep -e 'python' -e 'linux' data1.txt
linux is a easy language.
linux is a os.
python is a programming language.

   OR(grep -e 'python' -e 'linux' data1.txt/egrep -i "(python|linux)" data.txt

[ec2-user@ip-172-31-1-83 ~]$ egrep -i "(python|linux)" data1.txt
linux is a easy language.
linux is a os.
python is a programming language.











